WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.440
Hi my name is Sydeaka Watson.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.580
And so that's me.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.600
I work as a data
scientist at Eli Lilly,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.490
the pharmaceutical company.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.250
I'm on the development team
for a Shiny application

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.530
that is used at Lilly.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:27.330
So essentially my job is to
wear this hat, the app developer

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:28.830
hat.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.910
And this isn't just
the normal Shiny app.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.750
This is a complex
production-grade app.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.050
We get a new version of the
app released every month

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.750
with new features and
bug fixes, and a lot

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.780
of people in my organization
visit this app every day,

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.870
so it's really important that
the app is functioning properly

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.065
all the time.

00:00:47.065 --> 00:00:48.690
So with that in mind,
I'd like to begin

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.350
with a story about something
that happened to me back in May

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:53.250
2021.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:54.900
So I was in my
home office working

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.030
on a new feature for the app.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.060
But of course I was
multitasking because I was also

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.190
keeping an eye on the activity
in our team chat window.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.640
So one of the users was
saying she was having

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.500
some issues with the app.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.800
It wasn't populating the
dropdown menu with the values

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:11.890
that she was expecting.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.670
So the values were in the app
before and then all of a sudden

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.440
she didn't see them anymore.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.873
So one of the other developers
identified the issue

00:01:20.873 --> 00:01:23.040
and determined that we would
have to quickly release

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.590
a new version of the
app that resolved

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.210
the issue with
the dropdown menu.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.030
And that's when I
panicked because I

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:34.470
realized that meant two
things were about to happen.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.080
First it meant.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.040
First it meant that I would have
to put on another hat, my code

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:41.820
reviewer hat.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.730
So as a code reviewer, I'm
responsible for reviewing

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.550
GitHub pull requests. I have
to read the code to make sure

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.860
it adheres to our
coding standards.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.140
And then I have to run the
app in a local environment

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.150
to make sure that all
the old features work

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.830
with the new features.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.830
I have to check the radio
buttons, the dropdown menus,

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.810
the contents of the tables.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.990
I have to make sure that the
database API and the model API

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.540
connections are still working
as they used to, right,

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.590
as we added these new features.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:11.550
And so if it passes those
tests, then we deploy the app

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.650
to the test server.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:17.610
But then that means I have to
put on yet another hat, right?

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.560
This time I have to put
on my app tester hat.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.740
So as a member of
the app testing team,

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.250
I was one of several people
that had to manually deploy

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.150
the deploy, the deploy.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.870
I had to manually test the
deployed version of the app.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.480
So I would have to open
the app in a web browser

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.620
and then run a lot of
the same tests as before.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:38.430
Check the radio buttons,
check the dropdown menus,

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.920
check the API connections,
database connections,

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.430
all those other things that I
did before in the code review.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.900
And so now the
only difference is

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.858
that this is on the
deployed version of the app

00:02:47.858 --> 00:02:50.640
as opposed to the pre
deployment version.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.300
Now testing is very important.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.680
It does allow us to catch
bugs so that we can fix them

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.690
before the new version of the
app is released to production.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:02.190
However, as one of the
people that had to,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.890
you know, who was responsible
for actually running the tests,

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:08.580
I could say that this was
a really extremely tedious,

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.290
painful experience for me.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.290
It was very stressful.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.250
I also felt under-valued, right?

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:18.810
Because I'm thinking, OK,
I have a PhD in statistics

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.750
and yet my job now is to click
this radio button, right?

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.720
Or check this dropdown menu.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.820
So I felt like this wasn't
a good use of my time.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.000
Another issue is that I
and the other app testers

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.980
had other priorities, so we
weren't full time app testers.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.310
We could only run
the tests when we

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.800
had time to squeeze testing in.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.260
And that meant that managing
all of those testing

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.477
those schedules across the
different testers meant that it

00:03:43.477 --> 00:03:45.810
could take a long time for
us to get through those tests

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:49.140
and actually go to the next
stage of our software release.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.950
So because so that was
increasing the amount of time

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.390
it took us to deploy the app.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:56.980
Because it was so
time intensive,

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.450
we had to sparingly
apply these tests.

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:01.040
So we couldn't
afford to run them

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.070
as new features were
coming in, we had to

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:04.790
or use multiple scenarios.

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:08.920
We can only do them really once,
you know, if we could do that.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:12.070
And then lastly, we found
that different testers

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:15.435
were running the tests with
different conditions, slightly

00:04:15.435 --> 00:04:16.310
different conditions.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:20.170
So there was some subjective
assessment with whether or not

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:21.800
the tests passed or failed.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:23.423
So one tester
could say it passed

00:04:23.423 --> 00:04:25.090
and another person
running the same test

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:26.350
would say that it failed.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:28.640
So that was a problem.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.160
So because these releases were
such a heavy burden for me,

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.140
I started looking
for a way to make

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.500
this process more efficient
for me and for our team.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:38.640
I was already familiar
with Selenium.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.060
I knew it was a great tool
for automating interactions

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:42.860
with a web browser,
and I figured

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:45.350
this might help automatically
check whether the UI

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.320
elements were still working
on the deployed version

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:49.280
of the app.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.700
I was somewhat familiar
with the rvest package

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.500
and that it was great
for web scraping,

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.230
so I figured this might help
to programmatically extract

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.330
the contents of
the web page and I

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:00.987
could run some sanity
checks against it

00:05:00.987 --> 00:05:03.320
to make sure everything's
rendering properly. So Selenium

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:05.653
and our rvest could work
together on the deployed version

00:05:05.653 --> 00:05:07.240
of the app.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.670
My colleague Eric
Nantz recommended

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:11.980
that I use the
shinytest package

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.800
to test the code that other
developers were submitting

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.540
in their pull requests.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:19.030
So he figured that
might help to automate

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:21.017
some of the code reviews.

00:05:21.017 --> 00:05:22.600
And then, of course,
later I found out

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:26.020
that there was another version
of the shinytest package

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.300
called shinytest2
that somebody

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:29.770
will speak about later.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:32.410
And then finally, the
shinytest documentation

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:36.590
was recommending that we also
use the testthat package.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:39.010
And so now I had
all these tools. There

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:40.510
was a bit of a
learning curve, but I

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.070
took about three
months of my time

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:44.380
and eventually over
the next three months,

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:48.310
I was able to use those tools
to create an automated testing

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:50.380
pipeline.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.380
Let's see how the software
release workflow changed

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.800
after introducing these tools.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.930
So in the code
review stage in blue,

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:04.330
we see the manual pre-deployment
testing during code reviews

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:07.600
was replaced with the
shinytest2 script that

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.320
automates these same tasks.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:11.260
And now the code
reviewer hat sits

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:15.220
on the head of the bot
that runs the test script.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:19.810
And then in orange, in the
post deployment testing stage,

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:24.250
I'm running the manual tests,
the manual regression tests,

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:27.530
I'm replacing those with the
suite of Selenium test scripts.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:29.740
And then I get to remove
that heavy app tester

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:31.660
hat from my head and
place it on the bot who

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:33.910
is happy to run
those test scripts.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:36.190
At least he doesn't complain
about it if he does not

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.140
feel happy about it.

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.750
So those two changes
had a significant impact

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:42.830
on our software
release workflow.

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:46.867
Our code reviewers and app
testers had less work to do.

00:06:46.867 --> 00:06:49.200
They weren't feeling stressed
out with each new release.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.060
We didn't have to worry about
testers balancing their testing

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:53.750
duties with other
high priority work.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.290
So we ended up shortening
our time to deployment

00:06:56.290 --> 00:07:00.970
from five days to five hours,
which was pretty, pretty

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:03.070
huge for our testing team.

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:06.585
We could apply our testing
criteria as often as needed.

00:07:06.585 --> 00:07:07.960
So no matter how
many releases we

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:12.070
had during the month
or a feature feature

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:14.800
pull request that we
merged during the month

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:16.450
And then because
the test logic was

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:18.370
contained in the test script,

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:21.460
that meant we could
consistently apply those

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:24.370
that test logic from
one test to one,

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:25.820
from one test run to the next.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:27.850
So that removed that
variation in test results

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:30.130
that we were looking for.

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:32.590
I've talked about this before
on the previous slides,

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:34.450
but here's where I get
to formally introduce

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:38.100
the automated testing
pipeline that I'm proposing.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:39.970
It contains four key principles.

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:42.993
First, I believe you should
automate the tests, right?

00:07:42.993 --> 00:07:44.410
You should actually
have some sort

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:48.580
of programmatic representation
of your test logic.

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:51.520
And this could obviously
be we could easily

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.553
fill an hour or maybe a
day talking about this,

00:07:53.553 --> 00:07:54.970
but lots of different
things like,

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:57.050
you know, how do you
recover safely from errors?

00:07:57.050 --> 00:07:59.200
How do you appropriately
log the test results?

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.700
How do you have this repository
of all your test artifacts,

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:03.640
maybe your screenshots
or things that

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:05.730
were downloaded in the
course of running the app?

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:07.690
You know, that's what
I mean by automating

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:09.460
the test. So all of that.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:13.900
Next, it's important to have
one or more development servers

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:16.840
that are completely separate
from your production server,

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:19.240
and that allows us to see
how the updated app works

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:22.510
without breaking the production
version that end users are

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:23.770
interacting with.

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:25.870
And then the next
two principles have

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:28.250
to do with when we
would run the test.

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:31.030
The first is that I
recommend running the tests,

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:32.740
running some
pre-deployment tests,

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:35.440
so that you can integrate
these features safely,

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:37.960
you know, in a safe space
on your development server

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:39.940
without crashing the
version of your app

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:42.059
that the end users are using.

00:08:42.059 --> 00:08:44.920
And then finally,
I would recommend

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:48.970
running the
post-deployment check also

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:50.360
on the development server.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:52.383
And this is where
we can more closely

00:08:52.383 --> 00:08:53.800
mimic what the end
users are going

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:57.680
to experience when they log into
the app using the web browser.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.740
So again, pre deployment
and post deployment

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:02.750
that that's really important.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:05.060
One question that comes
up is whether automation

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.560
makes sense for a particular use
case or a particular web app.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.890
And to answer this question,
I usually follow up

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:14.560
with a couple of
follow-up questions.

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:17.870
The first would be, is
the logic consistent?

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:20.420
The test logic, is
it consistent, you

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:22.700
know, from one run to the next?

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.430
So if you can say your step
one, step two, step three,

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:28.790
if you can actually
have all of that logic

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:31.790
and not have to worry about it
changing from one to the next,

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:33.043
that's one consideration.

00:09:33.043 --> 00:09:34.460
And then the second
question would

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:38.255
be whether the test logic
can be completely scripted

00:09:38.255 --> 00:09:40.130
so that you can get the
result without having

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:41.840
to use any sort of
subjective judgment

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:44.900
or having some sort of human
involved making that decision.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:46.650
There are other
considerations, obviously.

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:48.230
So there's, do you have
the infrastructure

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:50.330
to support these automation
tools like shinytest2

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:52.010
or Selenium?

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:53.427
Do you have time
to do this, right?

00:09:53.427 --> 00:09:55.802
There's a little bit of a
learning curve and a little bit

00:09:55.802 --> 00:09:58.422
of time investment and in terms
of writing these test scripts.

00:09:58.422 --> 00:10:00.380
But in general, I would
say if you answered Yes

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:01.940
to both of those
questions, then I

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:04.920
would say your app is a prime
candidate for automation.

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.670
So I prepared a working example
that you can play around

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:12.740
with on your own.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:15.300
It's a simple app that allows
you to create an ice cream

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.850
recipe using various ingredients
like peanuts and raisins,

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:19.820
and chocolate syrup.

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:22.170
And for the adventurous, if
you want to add some chicken

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:25.260
nuggets to your ice
cream, no judgment here,

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.090
but we allow you to do that.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:30.870
So the GitHub repo, the GitHub
repo linked at the bottom

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:32.750
has all the code
needed to run the app.

00:10:32.750 --> 00:10:36.058
It also has the shinytest2
and the Selenium code

00:10:36.058 --> 00:10:38.100
that will allow you to
just play around with this

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:39.130
in your own environment.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:42.230
So I definitely encourage
you to check it out.

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:44.810
I created a couple of screen
casts that demonstrate

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:45.750
how this would work.

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:47.458
So first I'll show
you the pre deployment

00:10:47.458 --> 00:10:50.030
testing with shinytest2.

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:54.140
Now if you're running the
shinytest script in the RStudio IDE,

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:57.380
I'll pause this. First, you'll
see that there's a run test

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:59.360
button visible at the
top of the script,

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:02.240
and if you click that,
you'll see that there's

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:03.770
some activity
happening over here

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:07.580
in the Build tab that shows
the progress of the test run.

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:09.470
This test script
automatically opens up

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:12.920
this local version of the
app in a web browser window.

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:13.890
So I didn't do that.

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:15.930
It did it on its own
in Google Chrome.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:19.100
And then you'll notice that it's
selecting various checkboxes

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:21.610
and entering text
in the text field

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:22.860
and doing all kinds of things,

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:24.950
and showing how the
app is changing.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:27.270
So all of those things
happened automatically.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.710
And so when I go back
to the RStudio IDE,

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:32.640
I see that the
test result folder.

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:37.070
Now contains a few files that
were saved in the test run.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:38.900
And if I click on
one of those, I've

00:11:38.900 --> 00:11:42.260
got some screenshots
that show, you know,

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:44.690
what the app looked like at
various points in the app.

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:48.200
There's also this
JSON file that shows

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:50.180
the values of various
elements in the UI

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.010
as they were at the time that
the snapshot was created.

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:55.760
If you stick around for the
next talk in this session,

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:58.020
you'll actually get a
chance to see the package

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:01.460
author of, you know, the
shinytest2, a developer

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:03.400
give a presentation
about shinytest2.

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:05.510
So I didn't spend a whole
lot of time thinking

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:07.103
about that for
this presentation,

00:12:07.103 --> 00:12:09.020
but I highly recommend
that you check that out.

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:10.520
And now finally,
I'll show you what

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:13.610
the post-deployment
version of the testing

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:15.470
looks like with Selenium.

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:19.520
So as before, we click the
Run Test button at the top,

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:24.560
we will also see the test
run progress in the Build tab

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:26.180
in the upper right corner.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:29.510
Now, before we would actually
open up automatically this web

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:32.360
browser in Google Chrome, but
this is a headless browser,

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:34.560
so we don't actually have
access to that browser.

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:36.560
Instead, it's just going
to do it under the hood

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:40.970
and silently execute those test
instructions that I provided.

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:44.240
And so we'll just see the.

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:47.995
We'll see the mouse that I can't
see anymore for some reason.

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:55.323
Where are you, mouse?

00:12:55.323 --> 00:12:57.615
I see it on your side, but
not my side for some reason.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:05.460
Let me mouse.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:06.920
OK there we go.

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:08.880
OK, I'll pause that here.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.370
So what you saw and
unfortunately, I

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:14.190
wasn't able to narrate that
while it was going while I was

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:20.310
looking for my mouse, was
that it was showing you

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:22.470
how it was actually
programmatically running

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:23.250
the script.

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:26.880
And then it captured some
screenshots along the way

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:29.130
as it progressed through
the various test scripts

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:31.860
and it also in the log
area, it let you know

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:33.340
what was going on in the app.

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:35.160
So at this point
it was selecting

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:38.830
the flavor, and another point it
was selecting the recipe name,

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:40.560
and so on.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.842
So this was all very
easy and straightforward

00:13:43.842 --> 00:13:45.300
thanks to the tools
that, you know,

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.570
some of the other
developers have created.

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:49.500
So I thank them for that.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.690
So in closing, I'd
like to reemphasize

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:54.270
that these tools really
make automated testing more

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:56.610
accessible to the general
population of data

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.530
scientists, especially
people like me,

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:02.160
who don't have any formal
training in software

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:04.120
engineering or software testing.

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:06.660
Right? We're kind of
expected to know a lot

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:09.060
and do a lot about a
lot of different areas.

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:13.560
But this makes that a
lot easier for us to do.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:16.025
And then another thing that
is out of scope for this talk,

00:14:16.025 --> 00:14:17.400
but I get asked
this all the time

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:20.040
when I give a presentation
about automated testing,

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:22.532
you know, how does this fit
into the CI/CD pipeline?

00:14:22.532 --> 00:14:23.740
You know, it definitely does.

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:26.070
And there's lots of tools
that you can use to do that.

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:29.100
In particular, for example,
if you had GitHub Actions,

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:32.240
you could programmatically run.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:33.990
So as a person submits
their pull request,

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:36.720
you could programmatically
run your tests

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:38.850
on the pre-deployment
version of the app,

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:42.420
merge the pull requests,
deploy to the next test server,

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:44.340
and then actually
programmatically run

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:46.660
that next set of scripts.

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:48.410
Right? So you can do
a lot of these things

00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:50.590
made very, very easy.

